---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { PRESETS, limitText } from '../engine/presets';

/** The figure as the service writes it: whole MB for the big ones, KiB for exact binary sizes, else decimal KB. */
function human(bytes: number): string {
  if (bytes >= 1_000_000) return `${bytes / 1_000_000} MB`;
  return bytes % 1024 === 0 ? `${bytes / 1024} KiB` : `${bytes / 1000} KB`;
}
---

<Page path="/common-size-limits/">
  <h1>Common upload size limits, with sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every limit here comes from the service's own page, and each shows the date someone read that page. Limits change, so check the
    source before you rely on one. We never list a limit from memory.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Where</th><th>Limit</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Source (checked)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        PRESETS.map((preset) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{preset.label}</td>
            <td>{human(preset.bytes)}</td>
            <td>{preset.detail}</td>
            <td>
              <a href={preset.source.url}>{preset.source.title}</a> <span class="small">({preset.verifiedOn})</span>
              <br />
              <a href={`/?limit=${limitText(preset.bytes)}`}>Reduce to fit</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Reading these numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>MB and MiB.</strong> Where a site writes "MB" without saying which, we take the smaller (decimal) reading, so a
      file that fits here fits under either.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>KVGrainy's own box uses binary units</strong> (1 KB = 1,024 bytes). The "Reduce to fit" links above set it to just
      under the limit, never over.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A limit is a ceiling, not a target.</strong> A result a little under it is fine, and leaves room for rounding.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Some limits belong to your account,</strong> not the service: Gmail's differs for work and school accounts, and
      Discord says it is testing other limits. When in doubt, trust the service's current page over this one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    KVGrainy is not affiliated with, or endorsed by, Discord, Google (Gmail and YouTube), GitHub or Slack. Their names appear only
    to say which limit is meant; each number comes from that service's own page, linked in the table.
  </p>

  <p>
    Missing one you use? <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a>, with the page that states the limit, and we will add it once we have
    read that page ourselves.
  </p>
</Page>
